// Extern `alias` pass-through: a use-after-free until 2026-08-30, and now the
// regression guard for it.
//
// `let t = make(); let x = <extern alias>(t); return x` freed `t` at
// `passthru`'s scope exit and handed the caller the same pointer. `--verify`
// reported `release of a pointer that is not live`, 1 violation, and `println`
// printed an empty line because the string was already gone.
//
// **It is asserted rather than merely run, and that is the point.** It lived
// under `aif/evidence/xlang/` until that tree was superseded on 2026-09-03, where
// `run_corpus_test` built and ran it and checked the exit status -- which this
// defect does not change. `run_aif_verify_test`'s table names it now, so the
// assertion is the ledger: 0 leaked and, the number that matters here, 0
// violations.
//
// **Why, narrowed by three controls.** `return t` with no alias hop was clean;
// the alias hop with the value used locally and never returned was clean; only
// the two together failed. So the union in src/aif/walk.psm was firing all
// along -- `x` really does alias `t` -- and `return x` really did transfer to
// the caller. What did not happen was `t` losing its local scope drop, so one
// allocation had two owners.
//
// The guard that should have caught it is `irValueAliasesName` in
// src/ir/expr.psm, whose own comment describes this exact shape one callee kind
// further in (`let x = passthru(t); return x`, tests/test_85). It asked
// `aif_fn_may_return_param`, which answers no for an extern -- deliberately,
// because widening it for *unknown* symbols leaks one String per integer printed
// through std/io.psm and took three suite fixtures with it. The fix reads the
// **declared** contract instead: a written `alias` is a stated fact about one
// function, an unknown symbol is an abstention about all of them.
//
// `prismio_expect` is declared under its real symbol rather than through the
// `expect` builtin, because that builtin is the one name aifFfiAliasOf already
// special-cases -- so going through it would prove nothing.
import std.io
import std.string

extern fn prismio_expect(p: String borrow) -> String alias

fn make() -> String {
    return "hello".concat(" world")
}

fn passthru() -> String {
    let t = make()
    let x = prismio_expect(t)
    return x
}

fn main() -> Int {
    let r = passthru()
    println(r)
    return 0
}
